0.6.0:
- [x] Add unified login based on LlamaPress.ai mothership for better UX on login across LlamaBot & LlamaPress-Simple Rails apps.

0.6.0b: 
- [x] Fix UI/UX issues with our chat tab, so our background runs route properly and the user is less confused. (Ticket -> Engineer mode in particular was rough & broken).
- [x] Auto change to implement when it goes from ticket -> engineer mode.

0.6.0c: 
- [x] Make sure tool telemetry and errors don't get dropped 
- [x] Bound delegated sub-agent runs to 240s, disable hidden provider retries, stream progress heartbeats, and warn users after 90s of silence.
- [x] Add "fix_permissions" tool call to all other modes - beginner planning, engineer planning, ticket planning, etc.
- [x] Fix the branding window to move the labels below the hex code.
- [x] Add GPT 5 Nano as the default image vision model
- [x] Add compose button to be outside hamburger menu as top level item
- [x] Make collapse icon more clear
- [x] Add a field to type feedback when user clicks "bad" on the banner at bottom of how is leo doing?
- [x] Remove Leonardo Said: if they're copying a subset of a message, or just a single message, and include it if they're copying across multiple messages
- [x] Add a "reply" button so the user can quote Leo and respond to a specific, individual message.
- [x] Add the fix_permissions tool call into the other items.
- [x] Remove the deprecated search_tool toolcall, in liue of using glob and grep.

0.6.0d:
- [x] Add better persistence and retries for network errors
- [x] Add .ico as an allowed upload item in our file upload
- [x] UI/UX fix new button responsiveness on small horizontal width for chat
- [x] Add DeepSeek v4 flash endpoints for Fireworks, GMI, and Azure Foundry
- [x] Add resilient retries in LlamaBot (for websocket disconnections, etc.)
- [x] Add auth for Rails access reusing existing Gem functionality
- [x] langgraph.json in LlamaBot becomes source of truth for Platform nodes. (overrides what's in langgraph.json in Leonardo upstream repo)
- [x] Improve conversation history mechanics & reloads. (offload to s3 option, compact/summarize postgres, keep lighter weight human/ai message pairs for cold restore (>48 hours))

0.6.0e:
- [x] Add advanced JavaScript tool calling for Leo to execute tool calls JavaScript on the browser (disabled by default).
- [x] Improve the update banner experience: honest fail-fast errors (no more 3-minute dead wait), per-version failure marker suppresses the banner after a failed attempt (auto-unsticks on next release), failures reported to mothership via report_error, update UI hidden from roles that can't update.

0.6.0f:
- [x] Fix the leo custom modes not saving the cookie for last used mode. (UX issue)

0.6.0g: (inbox sweep — 10 delegated fixes, all failing-test-first)
- [x] Fix the dead "Update Now" button. It was broken on EVERY box since 0.5.3 (~45 running instances, cost us at least one churn): chat.html captured the modal/pill handles at parse time, but their markup lives ~370 lines further down, so both were null for the life of the page and openModal() silently no-opped. Handles are lazy now, and a qsRequired() helper warns instead of failing silently. (SI#203, supersedes SI#173/#74)
- [x] Fix scheduled-jobs /invoke leaking `idle in transaction` DB connections until the pool ran dry and every agent turn hung fleet-wide (crm box was silently dead ~40h). /invoke no longer holds a request-scoped session across the agent run — every DB touch is its own short-lived session. (SI#137)
- [x] Fix `delete_memory` killing the whole chat turn when the LLM passed a blank filename (IsADirectoryError), plus close the path-traversal hole in the same call. Bad tool input now returns a correctable error instead of raising.
- [x] Fix checkpoint Restore leaving every restored file root-owned, so Leo silently failed on the next edit to anything the customer had just restored. (SI: leo-palevi-dev)
- [x] Fix checkpoint Restore also reverting platform files (bin/update, docker-compose.yml, langgraph.json, applied migrations) — which left boxes running new images against an old platform and re-broke Engineer Mode. Restore now re-applies the bin/update ALLOWLIST paths from the pre-rollback HEAD as one labelled commit, and reports what it re-applied. (repeat of SI#179 on the same box)
- [x] Fix password login breaking whenever the agent enables authentication: protect_from_forgery ran AFTER the before_actions, so warden cleared the CSRF token before it was verified and the user was bounced back to the login page forever with the correct password. (SI#187, customer locked out for hours)
- [x] Fix the floating feedback bubble failing with "Failed to fetch" — it submitted via fetch() but the engine controller always redirected. The widget asks for JSON and the controller answers in kind; the HTML form is unchanged. (SI#139)
- [x] Unified Login: auto-provision (claim) SSO users on first sign-in for the Rails surface, so an authorized owner/operator's first "Sign in with your LlamaPress.ai account" actually signs them in instead of dead-ending. Opt-in via LlamaBotRails.provision_sso_users (on for managed Leo boxes, off for self-hosters). LlamaBot shadow users are now named by their verified email instead of lp-<guid>.
- [x] Add frontend error telemetry. Error reporting was backend-only, so a browser-side "Lost connection" mid-run left zero trace on the mothership. window.onerror / unhandledrejection / websocket-drop now POST to /api/frontend-error with source="frontend", deduped and capped per page load. Removed the dead report_disconnect scaffolding.
- [x] Attach debug context to thumbs feedback. A 👎 used to carry only thread_id/rating/note — no websocket close code, no model/mode, nothing to triage with. Feedback now ships a bounded, secret-redacted browser snapshot (ws close/reconnect, outbox, recent console), and websocket logs carry [ws=<id> thread=<tid>] so open/close/error lines can finally be correlated to the thread they served. (Mothership storage + admin display still to do.)
- [x] Centralize the Anthropic-only cache_control guard behind supports_prompt_caching()/invoke_with_cache() so no agent can reintroduce the fleet-wide TypeError crash on DeepSeek by copy-pasting an old invoke.
- [x] Paste an image straight into the purple feedback bubble (Cmd/Ctrl+V in the "Share your feedback…" box) — no save-to-disk-then-browse round trip. The attachment now previews as a thumbnail with its filename and an × to remove it, matching the camera-icon screenshot flow, and an image file copied from Finder/Explorer keeps its real name instead of being stamped `pasted-image-<ts>` (you can see WHICH image you attached). Nameless clipboard bitmaps are still stamped — browsers give them no name.
- [x] When the operator has vision turned off, images are now refused at attach time (file picker, paste, drag-drop and the screenshot capture button) instead of attaching a thumbnail Leo can't read and only failing at send. Non-image files are unaffected, and the refusal is explained in the banner above the composer (not a bottom-right toast), which reappears on every refusal instead of staying dismissed forever. The banner's × is finally visible: it was never really styled at all — the broad `.input-area button` rule outranks a single-class rule, so the × rendered translucent-white on faint purple (light gray on light blue) and no colour change had any effect until the rule was scoped under the banner. (QA: Michaela)
- [x] Attach several images to one feedback comment without posting it first. The file picker REPLACES the browser's selection, so picking a second screenshot silently dropped the first — the only way to get two images onto a comment was to post it and then edit it. Picks (and pastes) now merge into what's already staged, identical files aren't added twice, and every staged thumbnail carries an × so a wrong pick can still be dropped. Applies to the new-comment, reply and edit forms, and to the purple feedback bubble's paperclip — shift-selecting several images always worked, so the paperclip looked fine and the defect only showed up when you picked them one at a time. The bubble's existing test faked the picker by pre-seeding the transfer with what was already attached, i.e. it tested a browser that appends; it now models the real replace behaviour. (QA: Michaela)
- [x] Attach several images to one feedback bubble submission. The purple bubble kept exactly one screenshot and one file: a second screenshot replaced the first, and a second paste replaced whatever was picked before — so a bug that needs two pictures had to be filed twice. Screenshots, pastes and paperclip picks now all land in one scrollable list of thumbnails, each with its own ×, and all of them post on the single submission (the backend already accepted a list). (QA: Michaela)
- [x] See the element you selected, rendered, on the feedback item. Pointing the selector at a button and filing feedback used to show only its HTML source, so triaging "this button is wrong" meant reading tags. The feedback page now renders the capture in a live preview above the (still available) source. It's a fixed-height iframe that loads the same Tailwind/daisyUI/FontAwesome the dashboard uses, so the element looks about like it did on the page it came from, with an Expand toggle for a taller look — selecting something enormous scrolls inside the frame instead of blowing out the page, and a capture over 100 KB skips the preview and shows the source only. The frame is sandboxed without same-origin access and every `<script>` in the capture is dropped, so submitted markup can't touch the dashboard. (QA: Michaela)
- [x] Get from a feedback item back to the exact element it was reported on. The link to the originating page was a bare path behind a chain icon — QA couldn't tell it was a link, and following it dropped you on the page with no clue which element the report was about. It's now a labelled "Open page & highlight element" button, and the link carries the captured CSS selector: landing on the page scrolls to that element, rings it, and names the feedback item in a dismissable banner (with an honest "couldn't find it — the page may have changed" when the markup has moved on). The page path is also captured now even when no element was picked, so plain feedback links back to where it happened instead of nowhere; only same-app relative paths are ever linked, and the highlight params are stripped from the address bar on arrival so a copied URL is just the page. (QA: Michaela)
- [x] Remove an image from feedback while editing it. The edit form re-submits every existing attachment as a hidden field so a text-only edit doesn't wipe the files — with no control to drop one of those, the attachment set could only ever grow, and a wrong or sensitive screenshot could only be taken off from the show page. Each saved attachment now has its own thumbnail (so you can tell WHICH image it is) and a trash button that flips to undo, with removals applied on save. Removing the last remaining attachment works too — the form always submits the empty marker, so "delete them all" is no longer a silent no-op. (QA: Michaela)
- [x] On a full browser refresh, come back to the page the user was actually on. The chat page tracked the preview's current path in memory (fed by the Rails app's postMessage navigation events) but never persisted it, so every refresh — including the ones the update flow and a dropped websocket cause — dumped the user back on the app root and the "Your App" tab, losing their place mid-task. The last path and last tab are now remembered per Rails origin in localStorage and restored on load, including through the Unified Login consume redirect (return_to carries the saved page instead of a hardcoded "/"). Stored paths are sanitized before they can become an iframe src (a hand-edited "//evil.com" can't point the preview off-origin), an engineer-only tab is never restored for a `user` role (the role gate runs later, so it would strand them on a hidden tab), and a blocked/throwing localStorage degrades to exactly today's behavior. Manual QA checklist: docs/test_plans/0.6.0g.md.
- [x] Add friction reporting tool. Leos hit papercuts constantly — a tool erroring in a way its description never warned about, a root-owned file every edit silently fails on, output that contradicts the docs, a capability that isn't there and gets routed around — and none of it raises anything, so it died in the transcript and we only ever heard about it secondhand, from a customer complaining about the downstream symptom. Every mode now has `report_friction(what_happened, category, severity, tool_name?, evidence?, suggested_fix?)`: the agent files a short structured complaint about its OWN tooling, and it ships down the existing error pipeline (`MothershipClient.report_error` → the same InstanceError queue) tagged `source="agent_friction"` with `error_class=AgentFriction.<category>` — no new endpoint. Wired into engineer, engineer plan, beginner, beginner plan (the generic plan agent, so the database/ai_builder/testing/pyxl plan variants get it too), ticket and ticket plan, plus both delegated sub-agents, which run in isolated context and therefore see friction the main agent never witnesses. Three properties are pinned by tests rather than hoped for: it can never hurt the turn (bad enum values are coerced not rejected, a broken runtime is swallowed, and the POST runs on a detached thread so the agent never waits on it), it can't flood the queue (deduped by fingerprint, capped at 3 per conversation, sub-agents share the parent's budget), and it doesn't derail the agent — every return string including the dropped ones reads "noted, keep going" rather than as an error worth retrying. The instructions live in the system prompt, not just the tool description (a description alone doesn't get a tool used), and they're appended AFTER the project-context overlay so a mothership-delivered prompt override can't silently strip the instructions for a tool the agent still has. Mothership side still to do: `/api/leonardo/report_error` allowlists `source` to %w[llamabot rails_app frontend] and silently defaults anything else to "llamabot", so until that's widened these rows land wearing the wrong label (filter on `error_class LIKE 'AgentFriction.%'` meanwhile), and friction wants its own view — self-reported and subjective with no traceback, it wrecks triage if merged into the exception stream. Design notes: docs/dev/error_telemetry.md §4b. Mothership handoff task: docs/handoff_mothership_agent_friction.md. Manual QA checklist: docs/test_plans/0.6.0h.md
- [x] Fix graph recursion error bumping into 450 message limit

